issue_context.body:
(Made for [v1.3.x (features for extensions)](https://github.com/aseprite/aseprite/milestone/59) milestone)

When creating a dialog with default bounds, if the title is the width-defining element of that dialog's bounds, it will overlap with the close button, making the left part of it impossible to click on.

Adding a small margin when the title is the width-defining element of a dialog should fix the issue

Example script:

``` Lua

function generateDialogMain()
	DialogMain = Dialog{
		id="ThisIsAVeryLongNameForADialogDontYouThinkBecauseIdo",
		title="ThisIsAVeryLongNameForADialogDontYouThinkBecauseIdo"
	}

	DialogMain:separator{}
	DialogMain:separator{}
	DialogMain:separator{}

	DialogMain:button{
		id="ThisIsAVeryLongNameForADialogDontYouThinkBecauseIdo_button",
        text="OK"
	}

	DialogMain:show{wait=false}

	return DialogMain
end

generateDialogMain()
```

### Aseprite and System version

* Aseprite version: v1.3.9.2
* System: Windows

review_comments:
This is just a matter of personal taste, not an actual thing to fix. In this specific case I believe that a regular `if` clause would improve readability:
```suggestion
  if (titleLabel->bounds().x2() > mostLeftMiniButtonX) {
    gfx::Rect newBounds(titleLabel->bounds());
    newBounds.w = mostLeftMiniButtonX - titleLabel->bounds().x;
    titleLabel->setBounds(newBounds);
  }
```
